2026-08-12 18:03:38.503 | INFO     | cuga.sdk:sdk_callback_node:2129 - Routing to FinalAnswerAgent with answer: I’ll compute the exact finite-horizon probabilities, since late balls may have fewer chances than the early balls. First I’m checking the calculator’s supported evaluation format before applying the recurrence.
The basic recurrence gives a useful check value; I’m now evaluating the boundary-sensitive process across all 100 balls rather than assuming an infinite ramp.
The relevant comparison is positional: a tagged ball can only start in positions 1–3, and position 3 is best. I’m checking the resulting probability numerically as a consistency check, while the finite 100-ball boundary can only reduce opportunities for later balls.
Choose ball **3**.

A ball’s best chance occurs when it begins in the third platform position:

- If it is struck immediately: win, probability \(1/3\).
- If the first piston is position 1, it moves to position 2, whose win probability is \(5/9\).
- If the first piston is position 2, it moves to position 1, whose win probability is \(1/3\).

Thus the chance for a ball starting in position 3 is

\[
\frac13+\frac13\cdot\frac59+\frac13\cdot\frac13
=\frac{17}{27}\approx 62.96\%.
\]

Ball 3 starts in position 3 and has plenty of balls behind it, so it achieves this maximum probability. Every other ball starts in a less favorable position or has some chance of entering in a less favorable position.

**Answer: 3**
